\details{
\strong{Model (Sections 2 and 3.1, eq. 1-3).} The log death rates \code{y[x, t, i] = log(D[x, t, i] / E[x, t, i])} of each population \code{i} are centred over
the years: \code{m[x, t, i] = y[x, t, i] - a[x, i]}, with \code{a[x, i]} the mean
over the years. The centred rates follow
\verb{m[, , i] = beta kappa[, , i] + e[, , i]}. The age effects \code{beta} (ages x
\code{order}) are common to all populations; the period effects \code{kappa[, , i]}
(\code{order} x years) belong to each population. With one population the
model is the Lee-Carter model with \code{order} age-period terms, estimated by
a singular value decomposition; for \code{order = 1} it gives the fitted rates
and forecasts of \code{lee_carter(method = "svd", adjust = "none")}.

\strong{Identification (Section 2).} The columns of \code{beta} have length 1 and
are orthogonal to each other. The period effects of each population sum
to zero over the years, because the rates are centred. The sign of each
column of \code{beta} is chosen so that its sum is positive (package-specific).

\strong{Estimation (Section 3.2).} With \verb{Q[i] = m[, , i] t(m[, , i])}, \code{beta}
is the orthogonal matrix that minimises the sum over populations of the
squared off-diagonal elements of \verb{t(beta) Q[i] beta}: the paper's
least-squares modification of the common principal component analysis of
Flury (1984). Every population has the same weight whatever its size, as
in the paper, which prefers this to maximum likelihood because large
populations would dominate the age effects. The first \code{order} columns
are kept, and \verb{kappa[, , i] = t(beta) m[, , i]}. The paper requires more
years than age groups (Section 2), and so does \code{cae()}: with fewer years,
use wider age groups (\code{\link[=mt_regroup]{mt_regroup()}}) or fewer ages (\code{\link[=mt_subset]{mt_subset()}});
Kleinow fitted ages 18-52 and 53-87 separately.

\strong{Forecasts (package-specific).} Kleinow (2015) does not forecast. Here
the period effects of all populations and orders follow one multivariate
random walk with drift, \code{kappa[t] = kappa[t - 1] + mu + e[t]}, with normal
innovations whose covariance matrix is estimated from the year-to-year
changes (divided by the number of changes minus one), so the innovations
are correlated between populations and between orders. This is the
random walk with drift of Lee and Carter (1992) for every index, and the
multivariate random walk with drift that StMoMo uses for several period
indexes (Villegas, Kaishev and Millossovich 2018), here over all
populations together. Draws include the innovations and, as in
\code{\link[=lee_carter]{lee_carter()}}, the uncertainty of the drift (normal, with the innovation
covariance divided by the number of changes; StMoMo keeps the drift
fixed). \code{a} and \code{beta} are fixed. Forecasts start from the fitted rates of
the last year (\code{\link[=anchored]{anchored()}} starts them from the observed rates). Each
population has its own drift, so the forecasts of different populations
can diverge; \code{\link[=li_lee]{li_lee()}} and \code{\link[=coherent_fdm]{coherent_fdm()}} keep them together.
Predictive draws (\code{mt_forecast(predictive = TRUE)}) add Poisson variation
of the deaths given the rates and the exposures (the paper makes no
assumption about the distribution of the errors).

\strong{Other package-specific choices.}
\itemize{
\item The criterion is minimised by Jacobi rotations (Cardoso and Souloumiac
1996), each of which minimises it in one plane, started from the
eigenvectors of the sum of the \code{Q[i]}, until no rotation of more than
1e-12 radians lowers the criterion by more than 1e-20 times the sum of
squares of the matrices (at most 500 sweeps over all pairs of
columns; a warning of class \code{mt_warning_cae} reports when this limit
is reached). The paper uses
the F-G algorithm (Flury and Constantine 1985) with the modification of
Clarkson (1988). The criterion can have several local minima; the
estimate is the one reached from this start.
\item The columns of \code{beta} are ordered by the sum over populations of the
diagonal elements of \verb{t(beta) Q[i] beta}. The paper orders them by the
diagonal of one population and notes that the order can differ between
populations, with no general solution (Section 3.2).
\item Zero deaths are replaced by 0.5 before taking logarithms.
\item \code{common_by} (for example \code{"sex"}) gives each group of strata its own
common age effects, estimated from the strata of the group. The paper
names this extension and leaves it for further research (Section
3.1). The period effects of all strata still form one random walk, with
innovations correlated across groups.
\item If the data carry a hierarchy (see \code{\link[=mt_aggregate_strata]{mt_aggregate_strata()}}), \code{beta} is
estimated from the bottom strata only, and the aggregates get their
period effects by projection on it, as the other strata (as in
\code{\link[=li_lee]{li_lee()}}).
}

\strong{Choice of \code{order}.} The fit holds the mean squared error of the
centred log rates (eq. 4) and the approximate BIC of eq. (5), \verb{N log(MSE) + k log(N)} with \code{N} the number of rates and \verb{k = order (K T + n)} parameters for \code{K} populations, \code{T} years and \code{n} ages (one \code{beta} per
group of \code{common_by}); \code{\link[=mt_parameters]{mt_parameters()}} reports them (rows \code{mse} and
\code{bic}). In the paper's application (men in ten countries), \code{order = 2}
had the lowest BIC in both age ranges (Table 2). Compare orders also in a
backtest.
}
